```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on mswitch0, the multilayer switch acting as the Layer-3 default
gateway for all Campus 1 VLANs (VLAN 10 / 172.16.10.0/24, VLAN 20 /
172.16.20.0/24, VLAN 30 / 172.16.30.0/24). mswitch0's FRR ospfd process is not
running — confirmed by "ospfd is not running" from vtysh and the absence of any
ospfd process. Consequently, mswitch0 holds only four directly-connected kernel
routes covering its own VLANs and the 10.2.2.0/24 uplink to router16; it has
zero routes to the rest of the network (Campus 2 prefixes 10.5.0.0/24,
10.6.0.0/24, the backbone, etc.).

When PC5–PC14 send traffic toward Campus 2, packets reach their default gateway
(mswitch0 VLAN interface), mswitch0 finds no matching route, and immediately
returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly what a live ping from pc5
to 10.5.0.1 shows. Intra-Campus-1 traffic is unaffected because mswitch0 still
has connected routes for all three VLANs and performs inter-VLAN forwarding
locally. A stale OSPF LSA originated by mswitch0 (router-ID 172.16.30.254,
advertising 172.16.10/20/30) remains in the domain's database but will age out;
router16 confirms it has no OSPF adjacency with mswitch0, only with router7.

Router11 is entirely healthy — its RIB contains all Campus 1 and Campus 2
prefixes — and restarting it would have no effect. The root cause is that
ospfd was stopped (or never started) on mswitch0, eliminating its default
routing path.

Corrective fix: start ospfd on mswitch0 and configure OSPF with
  router ospf
    network 10.2.2.0/24 area 0
    network 172.16.10.0/24 area 0
    network 172.16.20.0/24 area 0
    network 172.16.30.0/24 area 0
This re-establishes an OSPF adjacency with router16, installs a default/upstream
route on mswitch0, and refreshes the VLAN prefix LSAs so downstream routers
continue to have correct return paths.
=== END ===
```